For children who are easy temperament, meet schedules, they're compliant, cooperative, they're emotionally regulated. Parenting, I think the parent-child relationship is going to have this same three conditions except contain and lead, but parents won't have to work as hard as deliberately, and it will be fairly easy to pull that off. When you have a challenging child, you have to be more intentional and conscious and explicit about what you're trying to do. Winnicott, who coined the term good enough mother, was a big fan of ordinary parents, ordinary mothers, in the sense that he saw in ordinary parents so many.

53
00:37:45.000 --> 00:38:21.000
strengths. And I think our field over time has sort of cast parenting as extraordinary, as something as a separate set of skills that you don't, you're not necessarily equipped with, that you have to go to some book or some expert for. We'd like to believe that if you can manage well your relationships, including the one with your child, you're going to be fine, but recognize that for some children, the task is going to be more challenging. And it's going I'll look a little different. Let's close with this one question. I'm going to throw it at both of you sequentially, which is for each of you, what is one

54
00:38:21.000 --> 00:39:04.000
piece of parenting advice that you would like to leave with our listeners? One of the things that I talk about within the beginning of the book, which when Tim approached me about writing this together, this is the first thing that came to my mind and really stuck with me when I was first learning how to be a clinical psychologist. So I'll give you, I'll give you this antidote and then I'll sort of end with what I mean by it all. So I had a parent come in and she had a young boy. And she leaned in close to me when she was trying to seek services. And she said, I love my child, but I don't like my child.

55
00:39:04.000 --> 00:40:07.000
And it stuck with me for so long and it took a long time for me to connect and understand what that meant finally when I had my own children. But I think the point being here that there are many things that you can work on as a parent. We can always do better and seek for more for our children. And yet if we can lead with love and if we can remember that at the end of the day, our task is to love our children and be there for them and accept them, I think that is the biggest thing that needs. needs to happen first before you worry about anything else. And so there are many things we want for our children. There are many things we hope for for our children. And if we just can accept them for who they are and where they're at, I think that is the first thing you have to accomplish as a parent before you can try to do anything else. Dr. Cavell? Parenting involves the head and the

56
00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:36.000
heart or science and art, if you will. And so some parents sometimes lead too much much with what's happening emotionally. And some parents are trying to do what the science they think is right. The science tells us this. There are some things that are important and try to emphasize those things. And our book speaks to that. And there are a lot of things that are not, that don't matter that much. And so if you want to use your mind or the science of parenting,

57
00:40:36.000 --> 00:41:01.000
recognize that not everything is consequential. In fact, the science of parenting would say to you very strongly, when you go to decide where to put your energies, put it on the relationship. That's where you double down. So if you're trying to decide should I put my child in time, not should I use a star chart, should I, you know, take him to a professional. Pay attention to the relationship. Is my relationship with my child healthy?

58
00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:39.000
Is it strong? Has it meeting that 80-20 criteria? And if not, what can I do to bolster that relationship? And I offer that because I don't think assessing the quality of the relationship is often what parents typically go to early. Instead, they're looking for a quick answer to fix some problem as opposed to saying, how are we doing my child and I? What can I do to make that relationship strong? Maybe do I need to, does it need to be repaired in some way? Am I struggling emotionally to be available and patient with my child?

59
00:41:39.000 --> 00:42:09.000
What do I need to do to be an emotionally healthy parent so I can have a better relationship with my child? So it's a different way to think about parenting, not as a job where you look at an endless series of fixes, but it's about a relationship that you manage 18 plus years. Well, I want to thank you both for joining me today. I think you have provided our listeners with some excellent and sound advice. Thank you. You're welcome. Thank you for having us. And speaking of our listeners, if you would like to be able to.

